\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop the mathematical theory underlying a class of scan experiments in
which each of finitely many indices produces a random number of \emph{hits}, and
each hit is recorded at a position within a scan window, binned into finitely
many bins. Such data decompose into a \emph{rate layer} (how many hits each index
produces) and a \emph{positional layer} (how those hits distribute across bins).
We prove that the two layers are uniquely determined by the expected occupancy
table; that the hypothesis of no interaction between them is exactly the
statement that the occupancy table has rank one, and exactly the statement that
all $2\times 2$ cross-product contrasts vanish; and that both the Pearson and
likelihood-ratio interaction statistics vanish precisely on this condition. We
prove that under homogeneity every pooled stratum profile is identical (so the
population Kolmogorov--Smirnov contrast between rate strata is exactly zero),
while in general the pooled total-variation contrast is bounded above by the
worst pairwise profile heterogeneity. On the rate side we prove a finite-mixture
law of total variance, deduce that conditionally equidispersed counts are
overdispersed with equality exactly at a degenerate rate layer, and exhibit
explicit models showing the two layers are logically independent. We then
determine the positional layer: the harmonic window law
$F_r(u)=\log(1+(r-1)u)/\log r$ is scale free, strictly front-loaded
($F_r(u)>u$ for $0<u<1$), identifiable from a single leading-fraction mass (the
map $r\mapsto F_r(u)$ is a strictly increasing bijection $(1,\infty)\to(u,1)$),
and is the exact limit of the discrete $1/j$ carrier: the normalised discrete
weight of the leading $k$ deciles of a doubling window converges to $F_2(k/10)$,
with leading decile $\log(11/10)/\log 2 = 0.13750\ldots > 1/10$. Finally we prove
finite-sample validity and strict positivity of permutation $p$-values, a
Bonferroni bound, nonexistence of the maximum-likelihood estimate on a separated
logistic design, existence and uniqueness of the ridge estimator for every design
and every $\lambda>0$, and a two-sided escape sandwich for the ridge estimator as
$\lambda\downarrow 0$.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The empirical setting}

A \emph{scan experiment} proceeds as follows. A finite family of indices
$i\in\iota$ --- in the motivating case, $128$ moduli --- is fixed. For each index
the experiment sweeps a window and records a finite set of \emph{hits}. Two
statistics are retained: the number of hits, and the position of each hit within
the window, discretised into $|\beta|$ bins (here ten deciles). The data set
contains $9{,}594$ hits.

Sorting indices into terciles by hit count --- hit-poor, mid, hit-rich, of sizes
$42/42/44$ --- yields the pre-registered question: \emph{does the shape of the
positional profile depend on the hit rate?} In the language below, does the
positional layer \emph{interact} with the rate layer?

Empirically the answer is no: an interaction likelihood-ratio test returned
$\chi^2 = 51.31$ on $49$ degrees of freedom ($p=0.383$; permutation $p=0.34$;
zero of $49$ Wald-significant bins), and the pooled hit-rich versus hit-poor
Kolmogorov--Smirnov statistic $D = 0.0462$, raw $p = 0.0038$, failed multiplicity
correction ($p_{\mathrm{adj}} = 0.049$ against a $0.05$ threshold). Meanwhile the
edge-decile excess replicated across all three terciles: $0.229/0.245/0.230$. A
control arm of the same design fired spuriously on dense size-matched controls
(permutation $p = 0.012$) with odds ratios clipped at $e^{\pm 30}$ --- the
signature of quasi-separation.

\subsection{What this paper proves}

A null result is only as meaningful as the mathematics surrounding it. We supply
that mathematics in four blocks: the structure of the two-layer model
(\S\ref{sec:model}--\S\ref{sec:algebra}); resolution and dispersion
(\S\ref{sec:resolution}--\S\ref{sec:rate}); the positional law
(\S\ref{sec:harmonic}--\S\ref{sec:discrete}); and inference
(\S\ref{sec:inf1}--\S\ref{sec:inf3}). All proofs are elementary and finite: no
asymptotic distribution theory, and no limits beyond one classical fact about
harmonic numbers.

\section{The two-layer occupancy model}\label{sec:model}

\begin{definition}[Two-layer model]
Let $\iota,\beta$ be finite nonempty sets (indices, positional bins). A
\emph{two-layer occupancy model} consists of a \emph{rate layer}
$\rho:\iota\to\R$ with $\rho_i>0$, and a \emph{positional layer} of probability
vectors $p_i:\beta\to\R_{\ge0}$ with $\sum_b p_i(b)=1$. Its \emph{expected
occupancy table} is $O_{ib}:=\rho_i\,p_i(b)$.
\end{definition}

Immediately $\sum_b O_{ib}=\rho_i$: the rate layer is the vector of row sums.

\begin{definition}[Pooled quantities]
Given weights $w:\iota\to\R$ and a stratum $S\subseteq\iota$, put
\[
\Pi_S(b):=\sum_{i\in S} w_i O_{ib},\qquad
m_S:=\sum_{i\in S} w_i\rho_i,\qquad
\hat p_S(b):=\frac{\Pi_S(b)}{m_S}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Homogeneity]
The model is \emph{positionally homogeneous} if $p_i=p_j$ for all $i,j$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[The layers are identifiable]\label{thm:ident}
If two two-layer models satisfy $O_{ib}=O'_{ib}$ for all $i,b$, then $\rho=\rho'$
and $p=p'$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Summing over $b$ gives $\rho_i=\rho'_i$. Then
$\rho'_i p_i(b)=O_{ib}=O'_{ib}=\rho'_i p'_i(b)$ and $\rho'_i>0$ permits
cancellation.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:ident} licenses the phrase ``the positional layer'' as a
statement about the data rather than a parametrisation.

\section{The algebra of ``no interaction''}\label{sec:algebra}

\begin{theorem}[Rank-one criterion]\label{thm:rankone}
Let $\iota$ be nonempty. There exist $u:\iota\to\R$, $v:\beta\to\R$ with
$O_{ib}=u_iv_b$ for all $i,b$ if and only if the model is positionally
homogeneous.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) Take $u_i=\rho_i$, $v_b=p(b)$. ($\Rightarrow$) Put
$\sigma=\sum_b v_b$. Summing $O_{ib}=u_iv_b$ over $b$ gives $u_i\sigma=\rho_i>0$,
so $\sigma\ne0$ and $u_i\ne0$. Then $u_i\sigma\,p_i(b)=u_iv_b$; cancelling $u_i$
gives $p_i(b)=v_b/\sigma$, independent of $i$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Interaction-free criterion]\label{thm:intfree}
The identities $O_{ib}O_{jc}=O_{ic}O_{jb}$ for all $i,j,b,c$ hold if and only if
the model is positionally homogeneous.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) Both sides equal $\rho_i\rho_j p(b)p(c)$. ($\Rightarrow$)
Cancelling the positive factor $\rho_i\rho_j$ gives
$p_i(b)p_j(c)=p_i(c)p_j(b)$. Fix $b$, sum over $c$, and use
$\sum_c p_j(c)=\sum_c p_i(c)=1$ to obtain $p_i(b)=p_j(b)$.
\end{proof}

Let now $O:\iota\times\beta\to\R_{>0}$ be a strictly positive table with
$R_i=\sum_b O_{ib}$, $C_b=\sum_i O_{ib}$, $T=\sum_i R_i$, $E_{ib}=R_iC_b/T$.

\begin{definition}
The \emph{Pearson} and \emph{likelihood-ratio} interaction statistics are
\[
\chi^2(O)=\sum_{i,b}\frac{(O_{ib}-E_{ib})^2}{E_{ib}},\qquad
G(O)=2\sum_{i,b}O_{ib}\log\frac{O_{ib}}{E_{ib}}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Sharp Gibbs term]\label{lem:gibbs}
For $a,e>0$, $a\log(a/e)-a+e\ge 0$, with equality iff $a=e$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
With $t=e/a>0$ the claim is $\log t\le t-1$, standard, with equality iff $t=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:stats}
$\chi^2(O)\ge0$ and $G(O)\ge0$, and each vanishes if and only if $O_{ib}=E_{ib}$
for all $i,b$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For $\chi^2$ each summand is nonnegative and vanishes iff $O_{ib}=E_{ib}$. For
$G$, since $\sum_{i,b}E_{ib}=T=\sum_{i,b}O_{ib}$ we may write
$\tfrac12 G(O)=\sum_{i,b}\bigl(O_{ib}\log(O_{ib}/E_{ib})-O_{ib}+E_{ib}\bigr)$, a
sum of terms nonnegative by Lemma~\ref{lem:gibbs}, each vanishing iff
$O_{ib}=E_{ib}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The test tests the intended hypothesis]\label{thm:testtests}
For a two-layer occupancy table, $O_{ib}=E_{ib}$ for all $i,b$ if and only if the
model is positionally homogeneous.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Here $R_i=\rho_i$ and $T=\sum_k\rho_k>0$. ($\Rightarrow$) $\rho_ip_i(b)=\rho_iC_b/T$
and $\rho_i>0$ give $p_i(b)=C_b/T$, independent of $i$. ($\Leftarrow$) If
$p_i\equiv p$ then $C_b=T\,p(b)$, so $E_{ib}=\rho_i p(b)=O_{ib}$.
\end{proof}

Combining Theorems~\ref{thm:stats} and~\ref{thm:testtests}: both interaction
statistics vanish precisely when the positional layer does not depend on the rate
layer.

\section{Resolution: what a null contrast bounds}\label{sec:resolution}

\begin{theorem}[Stratum invariance]\label{thm:stratinv}
If $p_i\equiv p$ then $\hat p_S=p$ for every weighting $w$ and every stratum $S$
with $m_S\ne0$. In particular $\hat p_S=\hat p_T$ for any two such strata.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\Pi_S(b)=\sum_{i\in S}w_i\rho_i p(b)=m_S\,p(b)$; divide by $m_S$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Zero population KS]
If $\beta$ is linearly ordered and $p_i\equiv p$, then for all $t\in\beta$ and all
strata $S,T$ of nonzero mass,
$\bigl|\sum_{b\le t}\hat p_S(b)-\sum_{b\le t}\hat p_T(b)\bigr|=0$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}[Pooling is convex]\label{lem:convex}
If $m_S\ne0$ then $\hat p_S(b)=\sum_{i\in S}c_i\,p_i(b)$ with
$c_i=w_i\rho_i/m_S$ and $\sum_{i\in S}c_i=1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Total-variation contrast bound]\label{thm:tvbound}
Let $w\ge0$ on $S\cup T$ with $m_S,m_T>0$, and suppose
$\tfrac12\sum_b|p_i(b)-p_j(b)|\le\varepsilon$ for all $i,j$. Then
$\tfrac12\sum_b|\hat p_S(b)-\hat p_T(b)|\le\varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
With $c_i$ (for $S$) and $d_j$ (for $T$) as in Lemma~\ref{lem:convex},
\[
\hat p_S(b)-\hat p_T(b)=\sum_{i\in S}\sum_{j\in T}c_id_j\bigl(p_i(b)-p_j(b)\bigr).
\]
Taking absolute values, applying the triangle inequality twice, summing over $b$,
exchanging the order of summation, and using
$\sum_b|p_i(b)-p_j(b)|\le2\varepsilon$ gives
$\sum_b|\hat p_S-\hat p_T|\le 2\varepsilon\sum_i\sum_j c_id_j=2\varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bin-wise version]
If $L\le p_i(b)\le U$ for all $i$ at a fixed bin $b$, and $w\ge0$ with
$m_S,m_T>0$, then $|\hat p_S(b)-\hat p_T(b)|\le U-L$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both pooled values are convex combinations of numbers in $[L,U]$ by
Lemma~\ref{lem:convex}, hence both lie in $[L,U]$.
\end{proof}

An observed pooled contrast is therefore a \emph{lower} bound on the worst
pairwise heterogeneity: a small observed contrast forces the per-index profiles
to be nearly identical. This distinguishes an informative null from an
underpowered one.

\section{The rate layer: overdispersion and independence of the layers}\label{sec:rate}

\begin{definition}
For weights $w$ with $\sum_i w_i=1$, conditional means $m$ and conditional
variances $v$, put $\bar m=\sum_i w_im_i$ and
$\Var(w,m,v)=\sum_i w_i(v_i+m_i^2)-\bar m^2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Law of total variance]\label{thm:ltv}
$\Var(w,m,v)=\sum_i w_iv_i+\sum_i w_i(m_i-\bar m)^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Expand $\sum_i w_i(m_i-\bar m)^2=\sum_i w_im_i^2-\bar m^2$ using $\sum_i w_i=1$
and substitute.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Overdispersion]
If $w_i\ge0$ and $v=m$ (conditional equidispersion, e.g.\ conditionally Poisson),
then $\Var(w,m,m)\ge\bar m$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:ltv} the excess is $\sum_i w_i(m_i-\bar m)^2\ge0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Equality case]\label{thm:equidisp}
Under the same hypotheses, $\Var(w,m,m)=\bar m$ if and only if $m_i=\bar m$ for
every $i$ with $w_i\ne0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A sum of nonnegative terms $w_i(m_i-\bar m)^2$ vanishes iff each term does.
\end{proof}

Thus any excess dispersion certifies genuine between-index rate variation.

\begin{theorem}[Unbounded overdispersion, homogeneous positional layer]
For every $C\in\R$ there is a two-layer model on two indices and two bins with
weights summing to one, all positional profiles equal, and
$\Var(w,\rho,\rho)-\bar\rho\ \ge\ C\,\bar\rho$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $s=4|C|+4$, rates $\rho=(1,1+s)$, weights $(1/2,1/2)$, common profile
$(1/2,1/2)$. Then $\bar\rho=1+s/2$ and the excess is $s^2/4$, which exceeds
$C(1+s/2)$ by the choice of $s$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Maximal positional heterogeneity, exact equidispersion]
There is a two-layer model on two indices and two bins with constant rates ---
hence $\Var=\bar\rho$ exactly --- whose two positional profiles are mutually
singular: $\tfrac12\sum_b|p_0(b)-p_1(b)|=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Rates $\rho\equiv1$, weights $(1/2,1/2)$, profiles $p_0=(1,0)$, $p_1=(0,1)$.
Constant rates give zero between-index variance; the total variation distance is
$1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
No implication holds in either direction between overdispersion of the rate layer
and heterogeneity of the positional layer.
\end{corollary}

\section{The harmonic positional law}\label{sec:harmonic}

\begin{definition}[Harmonic window CDF]
For a window ratio $r>1$ and $u\in[0,1]$,
\[
F_r(u):=\frac{\log\bigl(1+(r-1)u\bigr)}{\log r}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:cdf}
$F_r(0)=0$, $F_r(1)=1$, $F_r$ is strictly increasing on $[0,1]$, and for $u\ge0$
\[
F_r'(u)=\frac{r-1}{\bigl(1+(r-1)u\bigr)\log r}.
\]
\end{proposition}

The derivative identifies $F_r$ as the CDF of the harmonic density: substituting
$x=a(1+(r-1)u)$ turns $\mathrm{d}x/x$ into $(r-1)\,\mathrm{d}u/(1+(r-1)u)$, whose
normalisation over $[0,1]$ is $\log r$.

\begin{theorem}[Scale invariance]
For every $a>0$, $r>1$, $u\ge0$,
\[
\frac{\log\bigl(a(1+(r-1)u)\bigr)-\log a}{\log(ra)-\log a}=F_r(u),
\]
so two windows with the same endpoint ratio have identical positional profiles.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Strict early-window excess]\label{thm:excess}
For every $r>1$ and every $0<u<1$, $F_r(u)>u$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Strict Bernoulli's inequality for real exponents gives, for $0<u<1$,
$r^u=(1+(r-1))^u<1+u(r-1)$. Taking logarithms of both (positive) sides,
$u\log r<\log(1+(r-1)u)$; divide by $\log r>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Decile profile]
For $k=0,\dots,9$, $\pi_r(k):=F_r\!\left(\frac{k+1}{10}\right)-F_r\!\left(\frac{k}{10}\right)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
$\pi_r(k)\ge0$ for all $k$ and $\sum_{k=0}^9\pi_r(k)=1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Nonnegativity is Proposition~\ref{prop:cdf}; the sum telescopes to
$F_r(1)-F_r(0)=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Edge-decile excess]\label{thm:edge}
For every $r>1$, $\pi_r(0)=F_r(1/10)>1/10$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{definition}[Harmonic two-layer model]
Given any rate layer $\rho:\iota\to\R_{>0}$ and any $r>1$, the \emph{harmonic
model} has bins $\{0,\dots,9\}$, rate layer $\rho$, and the common profile
$p_i(k)=\pi_r(k)$ for every $i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Universality of the edge excess]\label{thm:univ}
In the harmonic model, for every weighting $w$ and every stratum $S$ with
$m_S\ne0$ we have $\hat p_S=\pi_r$ and $\hat p_S(0)>1/10$. Consequently any two
strata of nonzero mass have equal pooled profiles: the population
Kolmogorov--Smirnov contrast between hit-rich and hit-poor terciles is exactly
zero.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The harmonic model is positionally homogeneous, so Theorem~\ref{thm:stratinv}
gives $\hat p_S=\pi_r$; the excess is Theorem~\ref{thm:edge}.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:univ} is the mathematical content of the empirical pattern: an
edge-decile excess of $0.229/0.245/0.230$ in the three terciles together with a
non-firing interaction test --- one law, seen three times.

\section{Identifiability of the window ratio}\label{sec:ident}

\begin{lemma}[Strict convexity at the interpolation]\label{lem:xlogx}
For $r>1$ and $0<u<1$, writing $\sigma=1+(r-1)u=u\,r+(1-u)\cdot 1$, we have
$\sigma\log\sigma<u\,r\log r$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$x\mapsto x\log x$ is strictly convex on $(0,\infty)$ and $\sigma$ is the strict
interpolation of $r$ and $1$ with weights $u,1-u$; hence
$\sigma\log\sigma<u\,r\log r+(1-u)\cdot1\cdot\log1=u\,r\log r$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict monotonicity in the ratio]\label{thm:mono}
Fix $0<u<1$. Then $r\mapsto F_r(u)$ is strictly increasing on $(1,\infty)$, with
\[
\frac{\partial}{\partial r}F_r(u)
=\frac{\dfrac{u\log r}{1+(r-1)u}-\dfrac{\log(1+(r-1)u)}{r}}{(\log r)^2}>0 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The formula is the quotient rule; positivity is, after clearing the positive
denominators $r$, $\sigma=1+(r-1)u$ and $(\log r)^2$, exactly
Lemma~\ref{lem:xlogx}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Brackets]\label{lem:brackets}
For $r>1$, $0<u<1$: \emph{(i)} $F_r(u)\le u\,r$; \emph{(ii)}
$F_r(u)>1+\dfrac{\log u}{\log r}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
(i) $\log(1+(r-1)u)\le(r-1)u$ and $(r-1)/\log r\le r$. (ii) $1+(r-1)u>ru$ for
$u<1$, so $\log(1+(r-1)u)>\log u+\log r$; divide by $\log r$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bijection onto $(u,1)$]\label{thm:bij}
For fixed $u\in(0,1)$ the map $r\mapsto F_r(u)$ is a strictly increasing
bijection from $(1,\infty)$ onto $(u,1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Range containment: $F_r(u)>u$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:excess} and $F_r(u)<F_r(1)=1$.
Injectivity is Theorem~\ref{thm:mono}. Surjectivity: given $y\in(u,1)$, part (i)
of Lemma~\ref{lem:brackets} gives $F_r(u)<y$ for $r<y/u$, and part (ii) gives
$F_r(u)>y$ once $\log r>\log(1/u)/(1-y)$; continuity and the intermediate value
theorem supply $r$ with $F_r(u)=y$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The edge decile identifies the ratio]\label{cor:edgeident}
Since $\pi_r(0)=F_r(1/10)$, every edge-decile mass $m\in(1/10,1)$ determines a
unique window ratio $r>1$ with $\pi_r(0)=m$. Moreover, for $r,s>1$,
$\pi_r(0)=\pi_s(0)\iff r=s$ and $\pi_r(0)<\pi_s(0)\iff r<s$.
\end{corollary}

Thus the null hypothesis has an equivalent geometric formulation: two rate strata
have the same edge-decile mass if and only if they scan windows of the same
endpoint ratio.

\section{From the arithmetic carrier to the continuum law}\label{sec:discrete}

Write $H_n=\sum_{j=1}^n 1/j$.

\begin{lemma}[Window weight]
For $a\le b$, $H_b-H_a=\sum_{i=a}^{b-1}\frac{1}{i+1}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Cancellation of the Euler--Mascheroni constant]\label{thm:hdiff}
For positive integers $a,b$, $H_{aL}-H_{bL}\to\log(a/b)$ as $L\to\infty$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Classically $H_n-\log n\to\gamma$. Along $n=aL$ and $n=bL$, subtracting gives
$(H_{aL}-H_{bL})-(\log(aL)-\log(bL))\to0$, and
$\log(aL)-\log(bL)=\log(a/b)$ identically.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Discrete deciles converge to the harmonic law]\label{thm:disc}
For each $k\in\{0,1,\dots,10\}$,
\[
\frac{H_{(10+k)L}-H_{10L}}{H_{20L}-H_{10L}}\ \xrightarrow[L\to\infty]{}\
F_2\!\left(\frac{k}{10}\right).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:hdiff} the numerator tends to $\log((10+k)/10)$ and the
denominator to $\log2\ne0$. Finally $\frac{10+k}{10}=1+(2-1)\frac{k}{10}$, so the
limit is $F_2(k/10)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Edge decile]
$\dfrac{H_{11L}-H_{10L}}{H_{20L}-H_{10L}}\to F_2(1/10)=\dfrac{\log(11/10)}{\log2}
=0.137503\ldots>\dfrac{1}{10}$.
\end{corollary}

The numerator is exactly the total $1/j$ weight of the leading $k$ deciles of the
doubling window $(10L,20L]$: the discrete carrier realises the continuum law
decile by decile, and the observed edge excess is not a binning artefact.

\section{Inference I: exact validity of the tests used}\label{sec:inf1}

\begin{definition}[Permutation $p$-value]
Let $G$ be a finite nonempty set of relabellings and $t:G\to\R$ a statistic. Put
$p(g)=\#\{h\in G: t(g)\le t(h)\}/|G|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Finite-sample validity]\label{thm:perm}
For every $\alpha\ge0$, $\#\{g: p(g)\le\alpha\}\le\alpha|G|$; equivalently the
fraction of relabellings with $p\le\alpha$ is at most $\alpha$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $S=\{g:p(g)\le\alpha\}$; assume $S\ne\emptyset$ and pick $g_0\in S$
minimising $t$ on $S$. Every $g\in S$ has $t(g_0)\le t(g)$, so
$S\subseteq\{h:t(g_0)\le t(h)\}$, whence
$|S|\le p(g_0)|G|\le\alpha|G|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict positivity]
$p(g)>0$ for every $g$, since $g$ itself satisfies $t(g)\le t(g)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Bonferroni]
If $p_1,\dots,p_m$ each satisfy $\Pr[p_i\le\alpha/m]\le\alpha/m$, then
$\Pr[\exists i\le m: p_i\le\alpha/m]\le\alpha$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Finite subadditivity applied to $\bigcup_i\{p_i\le\alpha/m\}$.
\end{proof}

This is why a raw $p=0.0038$ adjusting to $p_{\mathrm{adj}}=0.049$ against a
$0.05$ threshold must be recorded as a non-firing.

\section{Inference II: separation destroys the maximum-likelihood estimate}\label{sec:inf2}

\begin{definition}
For a label $y\in\{0,1\}$ and score $z\in\R$, put
$\ell(y,z)=-\log(1+e^{-z})$ if $y=1$ and $-\log(1+e^{z})$ if $y=0$; for a design
$x_1,\dots,x_n\in\R^d$ with labels $y_i$, put
$\ell(\beta)=\sum_i\ell(y_i,\langle\beta,x_i\rangle)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Separation]
$w\in\R^d$ \emph{separates} the data if $\langle w,x_i\rangle>0$ whenever $y_i=1$
and $\langle w,x_i\rangle<0$ whenever $y_i=0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
$\ell(y,z)<0$ always; hence $\ell(\beta)<0$ whenever $n\ge1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Behaviour along a separating ray]\label{lem:ray}
If $w$ separates the data and $n\ge1$, then $t\mapsto\ell(tw)$ is strictly
increasing on $(0,\infty)$ and $\ell(tw)\to0$ as $t\to\infty$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
With $s_i=|\langle w,x_i\rangle|>0$, separation gives
$\ell(y_i,\langle tw,x_i\rangle)=-\log(1+e^{-s_it})$, strictly increasing in $t$
with limit $0$; summing preserves both properties.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No maximum-likelihood estimate]\label{thm:nomle}
If $n\ge1$ and the data are separated, $\ell$ has no maximiser.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A maximiser $\beta$ would satisfy $\ell(\beta)<0$, yet $\ell(tw)\to0$ by
Lemma~\ref{lem:ray}, so $\ell(tw)>\ell(\beta)$ for large $t$.
\end{proof}

On a separated design the reported coefficient vector is determined by the
optimiser's stopping rule, not by the data; any $p$-value or odds ratio computed
from it is uninterpretable. This is precisely the flagged control-arm behaviour:
apparent significance with coefficients pinned at $e^{\pm30}$.

\section{Inference III: the ridge repair and its escape rate}\label{sec:inf3}

\begin{definition}
For $\lambda>0$, the \emph{ridge-penalised} objective is
$\ell_\lambda(\beta)=\ell(\beta)-\lambda\|\beta\|^2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Concavity of the likelihood]\label{lem:conc}
Writing $\sipa(z)=\log(1+e^z)$ (softplus), $\ell(y,z)=yz-\sipa(z)$. Since
$\sipa$ is convex and the score is linear in $\beta$, $\ell$ is concave:
$\tfrac12(\ell(\beta)+\ell(\gamma))\le\ell\bigl(\tfrac{\beta+\gamma}{2}\bigr)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Strict concavity of the penalty]\label{lem:par}
By the parallelogram law,
$\bigl\|\tfrac{\beta+\gamma}{2}\bigr\|^2=\tfrac{\|\beta\|^2+\|\gamma\|^2}{2}
-\tfrac{\|\beta-\gamma\|^2}{4}$, strictly less than
$\tfrac{\|\beta\|^2+\|\gamma\|^2}{2}$ when $\beta\ne\gamma$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Strict concavity of the ridge objective]\label{thm:strict}
For $\lambda>0$ and $\beta\ne\gamma$,
$\tfrac12(\ell_\lambda(\beta)+\ell_\lambda(\gamma))<\ell_\lambda\bigl(\tfrac{\beta+\gamma}{2}\bigr)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Add Lemma~\ref{lem:conc} to $\lambda$ times Lemma~\ref{lem:par} (sign reversed by
the minus sign of the penalty).
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Coercivity]\label{lem:coer}
$\ell_\lambda(0)=-n\log2$, and since $\ell\le0$, any $\beta$ with
$\lambda\|\beta\|^2>n\log2$ satisfies $\ell_\lambda(\beta)<\ell_\lambda(0)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Existence and uniqueness of the ridge estimator]\label{thm:ridge}
For every design matrix --- separated or not --- and every $\lambda>0$, the ridge
objective has exactly one maximiser.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Existence: $K=\{\beta:\|\beta\|^2\le n\log2/\lambda\}$ is compact and nonempty and
$\ell_\lambda$ is continuous, so a maximiser $\hat\beta$ over $K$ exists; for
$\gamma\notin K$, Lemma~\ref{lem:coer} gives
$\ell_\lambda(\gamma)<\ell_\lambda(0)\le\ell_\lambda(\hat\beta)$ since $0\in K$.
Uniqueness: two distinct maximisers contradict Theorem~\ref{thm:strict} at their
midpoint.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The repair, stated against the failure]
On a separated design with $n\ge1$ the unpenalised maximum-likelihood estimate
does not exist (Theorem~\ref{thm:nomle}) while the ridge estimator exists and is
unique for every $\lambda>0$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:ridge}).
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Upper bound]\label{thm:upper}
Every maximiser satisfies $\lambda\|\hat\beta_\lambda\|^2\le n\log2$, i.e.
$\|\hat\beta_\lambda\|^2\le n\log2/\lambda$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Comparing with $\beta=0$:
$\ell(\hat\beta_\lambda)-\lambda\|\hat\beta_\lambda\|^2\ge-n\log2$, and
$\ell(\hat\beta_\lambda)\le0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Logarithmic cost of a near-perfect fit]\label{thm:logcost}
If $\delta:=-\ell(\beta)>0$ then for every observation $i$,
\[
\log\frac{1}{\delta}-\delta\ \le\ \|\beta\|\,\|x_i\| .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
All contributions are negative, so each dominates the total:
$\ell(y_i,z_i)\ge-\delta$ with $z_i=\langle\beta,x_i\rangle$. Writing the
contribution as $-\log(1+e^{-\tilde z_i})$ with $\tilde z_i$ the label-adjusted
score, this gives $e^{-\tilde z_i}\le e^{\delta}-1$, hence
$|z_i|\ge\tilde z_i\ge-\log(e^\delta-1)\ge\log(1/\delta)-\delta$, using
$e^\delta-1\le\delta e^\delta$. Cauchy--Schwarz gives
$|z_i|\le\|\beta\|\|x_i\|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Vanishing deficiency]\label{thm:defic}
If $w$ separates the data then for every $\varepsilon>0$ there is $\lambda_0>0$
such that every ridge maximiser with $0<\lambda<\lambda_0$ has
$-\ell(\hat\beta_\lambda)<\varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Comparing with the competitor $tw$ gives
$-\ell(\hat\beta_\lambda)\le-\ell(tw)+\lambda t^2\|w\|^2$. By
Lemma~\ref{lem:ray} choose $t$ with $-\ell(tw)<\varepsilon/2$; set
$C=t^2\|w\|^2$ and $\lambda_0=(\varepsilon/2)/(C+1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Escape]\label{thm:escape}
If $n\ge1$ and $w$ separates the data, then for every $M$ there is $\lambda_0>0$
such that every ridge maximiser with $0<\lambda<\lambda_0$ has
$\|\hat\beta_\lambda\|^2>M$; equivalently $\|\hat\beta_\lambda\|^2\to\infty$ as
$\lambda\downarrow0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fix an observation $i$ with $\|x_i\|>0$ (separation forces a nonzero score).
Choose $\varepsilon>0$ with $\log(1/\varepsilon)-\varepsilon>\sqrt M\|x_i\|$, and
$\lambda_0$ from Theorem~\ref{thm:defic}. Since $t\mapsto\log(1/t)-t$ is
decreasing, Theorem~\ref{thm:logcost} gives
$\|\hat\beta_\lambda\|\|x_i\|\ge\log(1/\delta_\lambda)-\delta_\lambda>\sqrt M\|x_i\|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The sandwich]
On a separated design with $n\ge1$: \emph{(i)} the unpenalised maximum-likelihood
estimate does not exist; \emph{(ii)} every ridge maximiser satisfies
$\|\hat\beta_\lambda\|^2\le n\log2/\lambda$ for every $\lambda>0$; \emph{(iii)}
for every $M$ there is $\lambda_0>0$ such that every ridge maximiser with
$0<\lambda<\lambda_0$ has $\|\hat\beta_\lambda\|^2>M$.
\end{theorem}

A ``significant'' odds ratio from a separated control arm is therefore not merely
unstable --- it diverges. The ridge repair converts that divergence into a
bounded, uniquely determined estimate whose size is an explicit function of the
penalty.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{(A) Two-layer decomposition and interaction diagnostics.}
Given a nonnegative occupancy table with positive row sums: compute row sums (the
rate layer), normalise rows to obtain profiles, compute the independence fit
$E_{ib}=R_iC_b/T$, and return $\chi^2$, $G$, and the maximal pairwise total
variation. Cost $\Theta(|\iota||\beta|)$ for the statistics and
$\Theta(|\iota|^2|\beta|)$ for the heterogeneity diameter. By
Theorems~\ref{thm:stats}--\ref{thm:testtests} both statistics vanish iff the
profiles are homogeneous, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:tvbound} the diameter
upper-bounds every pooled contrast.

\paragraph{(B) Inversion of the edge-decile mass to a window ratio.}
Given $m\in(1/10,1)$, solve $F_r(1/10)=m$ by bisection on $\log r$; by
Theorem~\ref{thm:bij} the map is a strictly increasing bijection onto $(1/10,1)$,
so bisection converges, with the brackets of Lemma~\ref{lem:brackets} supplying an
initial interval. Cost $O(\log(1/\mathrm{tol}))$ logarithm evaluations.

\paragraph{(C) Ridge-penalised occupancy regression.}
Maximise $\ell(\beta)-\lambda\|\beta\|^2$ by gradient ascent or Newton's method;
by Theorem~\ref{thm:ridge} the maximiser exists and is unique regardless of
separation, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:upper} iterates may be confined to the ball
$\|\beta\|^2\le n\log2/\lambda$ --- which doubles as a separation diagnostic.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

The occupancy table of a scan factorises into two separately identifiable layers;
``no interaction'' is exactly rank-oneness, and the standard statistics detect
exactly that condition. Under the null every rate stratum has the same pooled
profile, and in general the pooled contrast is dominated by the pairwise
heterogeneity, so an observed null is a quantitative bound rather than a shrug.
The rate layer is the unique source of overdispersion, and the two layers are
logically independent. The positional layer is the harmonic window law: scale
free, strictly front-loaded, identifiable from its edge decile, and the exact
continuum limit of a discrete $1/j$ carrier with leading-decile mass
$\log(11/10)/\log2=0.1375\ldots$.

Open directions. \emph{Identify the rate-variance carrier}: the unexplained
between-index dispersion is a property of the rate layer alone, and the positional
layer is now provably uninformative for it. \emph{A powered follow-up} on the
post-hoc mean-position contrast, whose sign flipped under matching.
\emph{Sharpen the escape rate}: the true rate is expected to be
$\|\hat\beta_\lambda\|\asymp\log(1/\lambda)$, between the logarithmic lower bound
and $\sqrt{n\log2/\lambda}$. \emph{Non-doubling windows}: extend
Theorem~\ref{thm:disc} from $r=2$ to every rational ratio by the same
harmonic-difference argument. \emph{Beyond total variation}: analogous resolution
bounds in $\chi^2$ or Kullback--Leibler divergence would yield a power calculation
from first principles.

